const COURSE = [{"id": 0, "group": "Python Basics", "title": "Your very first program", "intro": "You don’t need to know anything about coding. Start with one line.", "body": "<p>A <strong>program</strong> is a set of instructions for a computer. Python is a language for writing those instructions. People use it to automate tasks, analyze data, build websites, and research machine learning.</p><p>Python reads your instructions from top to bottom. <code>print()</code> displays something. The text inside quotation marks is called a <strong>string</strong>.</p><p>The parentheses hold what you want to print. A line starting with <code>#</code> is a comment: a note for humans that Python ignores.</p>", "example": "# This is a comment\nprint(\"Hello, World!\")", "task": "Print Hello, World! on one line, then print I can code! on the next. Match the capital letters and punctuation.", "starter": "# Say hello first\nprint(\"Hello, World!\")\n\n# Now add your second message\n", "answer": "print(\"Hello, World!\")\nprint(\"I can code!\")", "hint": "Copy the shape of the first print line. Put your new message between quotes.", "tests": [{"label": "Say hello", "expr": "len(output.splitlines()) > 0 and output.splitlines()[0] == \"Hello, World!\""}, {"label": "Add your own second line", "expr": "len(output.splitlines()) > 1 and output.splitlines()[1] == \"I can code!\""}, {"label": "Print exactly two lines", "expr": "len(output.splitlines()) == 2"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 1, "group": "Python Basics", "title": "Files, editors & running Python", "intro": "Understand where code lives, before worrying about setup.", "body": "<p>A file ending in <code>.py</code> is a plain text file containing Python code. An <strong>editor</strong> helps you write it; an <strong>interpreter</strong> runs it. An IDE combines an editor with tools for running and debugging.</p><p>This page includes an editor and a real Python interpreter, so installation is optional. To work on your own computer, download Python 3 from <a href=\"https://www.python.org/downloads/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">python.org</a>. On Windows, enable the installer’s PATH option if shown. Use the included IDLE editor or another editor such as VS Code.</p><p>Save a file as <code>hello.py</code>. In a terminal opened in that folder, try <code>python hello.py</code>, <code>python3 hello.py</code> on macOS/Linux, or <code>py hello.py</code> on Windows. The command depends on your installation.</p><p>Code runs in order. Quotes surround text, but do not appear in printed output. Comments explain why code exists.</p>", "example": "# hello.py\nprint(\"Step 1\")\nprint(\"Step 2\")", "task": "Print Ready, then Set, then Go! on three separate lines. Add a comment explaining the program.", "starter": "# Explain your program here\n", "answer": "# Count down to a start\nprint(\"Ready\")\nprint(\"Set\")\nprint(\"Go!\")", "hint": "Use three print() calls in the same order as the requested output.", "tests": [{"label": "Three messages in order", "expr": "output.splitlines() == [\"Ready\", \"Set\", \"Go!\"]"}, {"label": "Include a real Python comment", "expr": "any(t.type == __import__(\"tokenize\").COMMENT for t in __import__(\"tokenize\").generate_tokens(__import__(\"io\").StringIO(source).readline))"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 2, "group": "Variables", "title": "Names that remember values", "intro": "A variable gives a value a name you can reuse.", "body": "<p><code>age = 25</code> assigns the number 25 to the name age. The equals sign means <strong>assign</strong>, not “are these equal?” Assigning again replaces the value associated with that name.</p><p>Names can contain letters, digits, and underscores, but cannot start with a digit. Names are case-sensitive: <code>age</code> and <code>Age</code> differ. Use descriptive names like <code>study_hours</code>; avoid Python keywords such as <code>class</code>.</p><p>Basic types: <code>int</code> for whole numbers, <code>float</code> for decimal numbers, <code>str</code> for text, <code>bool</code> for True/False, and <code>None</code> for no value yet. True, False, and None need capitals and no quotes.</p>", "example": "name = \"Ada\"\ncredits = 3\nprice = 9.5\nis_ready = True\nresult = None\nprint(name, credits)", "task": "Create name with the string Ada, credits with the integer 3, is_ready with the boolean True, and result with None. Print name.", "starter": "name = \"\"\ncredits = 0\nis_ready = False\nresult = None\n", "answer": "name = \"Ada\"\ncredits = 3\nis_ready = True\nresult = None\nprint(name)", "hint": "Text needs quotes. Numbers, True, and None do not.", "tests": [{"label": "Name is the string Ada", "expr": "name == \"Ada\" and type(name) is str"}, {"label": "Credits is an integer", "expr": "credits == 3 and type(credits) is int"}, {"label": "Ready is a boolean", "expr": "is_ready is True"}, {"label": "Result has no value yet", "expr": "result is None"}, {"label": "Display the name", "expr": "output.strip() == \"Ada\""}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 3, "group": "Variables", "title": "Types & conversions", "intro": "The text \"12\" and the number 12 are different things.", "body": "<p>Use <code>type(value)</code> to inspect a value’s type. <code>int(\"12\")</code> creates a whole number, <code>float(\"2.5\")</code> a decimal number, and <code>str(12)</code> text. Conversion creates a new value.</p><p><code>bool()</code> converts to a truth value. Zero, an empty string, empty collections, and None are false-like. Nonempty strings—including <code>\"False\"</code>—are true-like.</p><p><code>int(3.9)</code> truncates toward zero; it does not round. <code>int(\"3.9\")</code> raises an error. You can first use float() if a decimal input is expected.</p>", "example": "text = \"12\"\nnumber = int(text)\nprint(number + 1)\nprint(type(number))", "task": "Convert raw_count to an integer named count, raw_price to a float named price, and count to text named label. Set empty_is_true to bool(\"\").", "starter": "raw_count = \"12\"\nraw_price = \"2.5\"\n# Convert these values below\n", "answer": "raw_count = \"12\"\nraw_price = \"2.5\"\ncount = int(raw_count)\nprice = float(raw_price)\nlabel = str(count)\nempty_is_true = bool(\"\")", "hint": "Call the type you want, with the existing value inside parentheses.", "tests": [{"label": "Integer conversion", "expr": "count == 12 and type(count) is int"}, {"label": "Float conversion", "expr": "price == 2.5 and type(price) is float"}, {"label": "Text conversion", "expr": "label == \"12\""}, {"label": "Empty text is false-like", "expr": "empty_is_true is False"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 4, "group": "Operators", "title": "Calculate & compare", "intro": "Turn values into answers using operators.", "body": "<p><code>+ - * /</code> add, subtract, multiply, and divide. <code>/</code> produces a float. <code>//</code> is floor division, <code>%</code> is the remainder, and <code>**</code> raises a power. For example, 17 // 5 is 3 and 17 % 5 is 2. Floor division rounds down, so -3 // 2 is -2.</p><p><code>+=</code> updates a value: <code>count += 1</code> means count = count + 1. Related operators include -=, *=, and /=.</p><p><code>== != &gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;=</code> compare values. <code>and</code> requires both conditions, <code>or</code> requires at least one, and <code>not</code> flips a truth value.</p><p>Precedence: parentheses, powers, multiplication/division/remainder, addition/subtraction, comparisons, not, and, or. Use parentheses whenever intent could be unclear.</p>", "example": "print(2 + 3 * 4)   # 14\nprint((2 + 3) * 4) # 20\nprint(8 >= 5 and 8 < 10)", "task": "You have 17 sweets and bags holding 5 each. Set bags to the number of full bags, leftover to the remainder, and enough to whether bags is at least 3. Set squared to 5 squared.", "starter": "sweets = 17\nper_bag = 5\n", "answer": "sweets = 17\nper_bag = 5\nbags = sweets // per_bag\nleftover = sweets % per_bag\nenough = bags >= 3\nsquared = 5 ** 2", "hint": "Use // for full groups, % for leftovers, and ** for powers.", "tests": [{"label": "Three full bags", "expr": "bags == 3"}, {"label": "Two left over", "expr": "leftover == 2"}, {"label": "Compare the bag count", "expr": "enough is True"}, {"label": "Calculate a power", "expr": "squared == 25"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 5, "group": "Strings", "title": "Working with text", "intro": "A string is an ordered sequence of characters.", "body": "<p>Single and double quotes both create strings. Use matching quotes. Indexes start at zero: <code>\"Python\"[0]</code> is P. A negative index counts backward: [-1] is the last character.</p><p>A slice <code>text[start:stop]</code> includes start but excludes stop. Missing start/stop uses the beginning/end. <code>len(text)</code> counts characters and <code>+</code> joins strings.</p><p>Methods transform or inspect text: <code>.upper()</code>, <code>.lower()</code>, <code>.strip()</code> (outer whitespace), <code>.replace(old, new)</code>, <code>.split()</code> (a list of words), and <code>.find(text)</code> (first index or -1).</p><p>Strings cannot be changed in place: methods return new values. An f-string inserts expressions: <code>f\"Hi, {name}\"</code>. Escapes include <code>\\n</code> for newline, <code>\\t</code> for tab, and <code>\\\"</code> for a quote.</p>", "example": "word = \"Python\"\nprint(word[0], word[-1])\nprint(word[:3])\nprint(f\"{word} has {len(word)} letters\")", "task": "Clean raw with strip() and title-style capitalization to produce Ada (use .capitalize()). Save it as name. Set greeting to Hello, Ada!, first to A, and length to 3 using your cleaned name.", "starter": "raw = \"  ada  \"\n", "answer": "raw = \"  ada  \"\nname = raw.strip().capitalize()\ngreeting = f\"Hello, {name}!\"\nfirst = name[0]\nlength = len(name)", "hint": "Chain methods: raw.strip().capitalize(). Use an f-string for the greeting.", "tests": [{"label": "Clean the name", "expr": "name == \"Ada\""}, {"label": "Build the greeting", "expr": "greeting == \"Hello, Ada!\""}, {"label": "Read the first character", "expr": "first == \"A\""}, {"label": "Count the characters", "expr": "length == 3"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 6, "group": "Strings", "title": "Ask for input", "intro": "Make a program that responds to its user.", "body": "<p><code>input(\"Your name? \")</code> asks a question and returns the answer as a string. Store it with <code>name = input(...)</code>.</p><p>Even if someone types 21, input() returns the text \"21\". Use <code>int()</code> or <code>float()</code> before calculating.</p><p>In this playground, expand <strong>Program input</strong> and enter one answer per line before running. Checks use their own fixed inputs so your experiments do not change the test.</p>", "example": "name = input(\"Name: \")\nprint(f\"Hello, {name}!\")", "task": "Ask for a name, then an age. Print exactly Ada will be 26 next year. when the answers are Ada and 25. Calculate the age instead of hard-coding the sentence.", "starter": "name = input(\"Name: \")\n# Ask for an age and convert it\n", "answer": "name = input(\"Name: \")\nage = int(input(\"Age: \"))\nprint(f\"{name} will be {age + 1} next year.\")", "hint": "Use int(input(\"Age: \")) to read a whole number.", "tests": [{"label": "Read the supplied name", "expr": "name == \"Ada\""}, {"label": "Convert the supplied age", "expr": "age == 25 and type(age) is int"}, {"label": "Print next year’s age", "expr": "output.strip() == \"Ada will be 26 next year.\""}], "inputs": "Ada\n25"}, {"id": 7, "group": "Conditions", "title": "Choose what happens next", "intro": "Conditions let a program take different paths.", "body": "<p><code>if</code> runs a block only when its condition is true. <code>elif</code> tries another condition; <code>else</code> handles what remains. Only the first matching branch runs.</p><p>A colon ends the condition line. Indent the block underneath with four spaces. Indentation tells Python which instructions belong together.</p><p>Use <code>==</code> for comparison and <code>=</code> for assignment. You can nest an if inside another block, or combine conditions with and, or, and not.</p>", "example": "score = 72\nif score >= 80:\n    print(\"Great\")\nelif score >= 50:\n    print(\"Passed\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Keep practicing\")", "task": "Set grade to A for scores 90 or higher, B for 80–89, and C for anything lower. Start with score = 85. Checks also try 90, 79, and 100.", "starter": "score = 85\n# Set grade using if / elif / else\n", "answer": "score = 85\nif score >= 90:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif score >= 80:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"C\"", "hint": "Check the highest threshold first. Each branch assigns grade.", "tests": [{"label": "85 earns B", "expr": "grade == \"B\""}, {"label": "90 earns A", "expr": "rerun({\"score\":90})[\"grade\"] == \"A\""}, {"label": "79 earns C", "expr": "rerun({\"score\":79})[\"grade\"] == \"C\""}, {"label": "100 earns A", "expr": "rerun({\"score\":100})[\"grade\"] == \"A\""}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 8, "group": "Loops", "title": "Repeat with purpose", "intro": "Let the computer handle repetition.", "body": "<p>A <code>for</code> loop visits each item in a sequence. <code>range(5)</code> gives 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. <code>range(1, 6)</code> gives 1 through 5. A third argument sets the step.</p><p>A <code>while</code> loop repeats while a condition is true. Update something inside it so the condition eventually becomes false.</p><p><code>break</code> exits the nearest loop. <code>continue</code> skips to its next iteration. <code>pass</code> does nothing—it is a placeholder, not a skip. A nested loop completes its inner loop for each outer iteration.</p><p>You can loop through a string character by character. An accumulator, such as total, starts before the loop and gets updated inside it.</p>", "example": "total = 0\nfor number in range(1, 4):\n    total += number\nprint(total) # 6\n\nfor letter in \"hi\":\n    print(letter)", "task": "Use a for loop to sum the integers from 1 through 5 into total. Use a while loop to reduce countdown from 3 to 0.", "starter": "total = 0\n# Add 1 through 5\n\ncountdown = 3\n# Count down to zero\n", "answer": "total = 0\nfor number in range(1, 6):\n    total += number\ncountdown = 3\nwhile countdown > 0:\n    countdown -= 1", "hint": "range() excludes its stopping value. In the while loop, subtract one each time.", "tests": [{"label": "Sum is 15", "expr": "total == 15"}, {"label": "Countdown reaches zero", "expr": "countdown == 0"}, {"label": "Practice both loop types", "expr": "has_node(\"For\") and has_node(\"While\")"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 9, "group": "Collections", "title": "Lists: keep things together", "intro": "A list is an ordered, changeable collection.", "body": "<p>Create a list with square brackets: <code>[\"tea\", \"milk\"]</code>. Read positions with [0], [-1], and slices, just like strings. Unlike strings, lists allow assignment: <code>items[0] = \"coffee\"</code>.</p><p><code>.append(x)</code> adds at the end; <code>.insert(i, x)</code> inserts at a position. <code>.remove(x)</code> removes the first matching value and <code>.pop()</code> removes and returns the last item (or an indexed item).</p><p><code>items.sort()</code> changes the list and returns None. <code>sorted(items)</code> returns a new sorted list. <code>len(items)</code> counts items; <code>x in items</code> checks membership.</p><p>Loop with <code>for item in items:</code>. Nested lists store lists inside lists: <code>grid[0][1]</code> reads row 0, column 1. Assigning a list to another name shares it; use <code>.copy()</code> for a shallow copy.</p>", "example": "items = [\"tea\", \"milk\"]\nitems.append(\"bread\")\nprint(items[-1])\nfor item in items:\n    print(item)", "task": "Start with scores = [70, 90, 80]. Append 100, remove 70, then sort the list. Set best to the last item and count to the list length.", "starter": "scores = [70, 90, 80]\n", "answer": "scores = [70, 90, 80]\nscores.append(100)\nscores.remove(70)\nscores.sort()\nbest = scores[-1]\ncount = len(scores)", "hint": "Call .sort() on its own line. Do not assign its result back to scores.", "tests": [{"label": "Update and sort scores", "expr": "scores == [80, 90, 100]"}, {"label": "Read the best score", "expr": "best == 100"}, {"label": "Count the scores", "expr": "count == 3"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 10, "group": "Collections", "title": "Tuples & unpacking", "intro": "Some groups of values should stay together without changing.", "body": "<p>A <strong>tuple</strong> is an ordered collection that cannot have its entries reassigned. Use <code>(3, 4)</code>. A one-item tuple needs a comma: <code>(3,)</code>.</p><p>Indexing and slicing work like lists. Use lists for collections you plan to change, and tuples for fixed groupings such as coordinates.</p><p>Unpacking assigns each entry to a name: <code>x, y = (3, 4)</code>. The number of names must normally match the number of values. Tuples can contain mutable objects, so immutability applies to their entries, not everything inside them.</p>", "example": "point = (3, 4)\nx, y = point\nprint(x, y)", "task": "Create a tuple named student containing Ada and 25. Unpack it into name and age. Create a single-item tuple named only containing Python.", "starter": "# Create and unpack your tuples\n", "answer": "student = (\"Ada\", 25)\nname, age = student\nonly = (\"Python\",)", "hint": "Parentheses alone do not create a one-item tuple. Remember its trailing comma.", "tests": [{"label": "A two-item tuple", "expr": "student == (\"Ada\",25) and type(student) is tuple"}, {"label": "Unpack both values", "expr": "name == \"Ada\" and age == 25"}, {"label": "A one-item tuple", "expr": "only == (\"Python\",)"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 11, "group": "Collections", "title": "Dictionaries: values with labels", "intro": "Look up information by a meaningful key.", "body": "<p>A dictionary stores <strong>key-value pairs</strong>: <code>{\"name\": \"Ada\", \"age\": 25}</code>. Keys are unique and must be hashable; strings and numbers are common choices.</p><p><code>person[\"name\"]</code> reads a value. A missing key raises KeyError. <code>person.get(\"name\", \"Unknown\")</code> supplies a default instead.</p><p><code>person[\"age\"] = 26</code> adds or updates a key. <code>del person[\"age\"]</code> deletes it; <code>.pop(key)</code> removes and returns its value.</p><p><code>.keys()</code>, <code>.values()</code>, and <code>.items()</code> provide views of keys, values, or pairs. Use <code>for key, value in person.items():</code>. A nested dictionary can be read with <code>students[\"Ada\"][\"grade\"]</code>.</p>", "example": "person = {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"age\": 25}\nperson[\"age\"] = 26\nfor key, value in person.items():\n    print(key, value)", "task": "Create a contact dictionary with name Ada and phone 123. Update phone to 456, add city London, and set missing to the result of looking up email with default Not provided.", "starter": "contact = {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"phone\": \"123\"}\n", "answer": "contact = {\"name\": \"Ada\", \"phone\": \"123\"}\ncontact[\"phone\"] = \"456\"\ncontact[\"city\"] = \"London\"\nmissing = contact.get(\"email\", \"Not provided\")", "hint": "Square bracket assignment updates or adds a key. Use .get() when a key might not exist.", "tests": [{"label": "Update the phone", "expr": "contact[\"phone\"] == \"456\""}, {"label": "Add a city", "expr": "contact[\"city\"] == \"London\""}, {"label": "Handle a missing key", "expr": "missing == \"Not provided\""}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 12, "group": "Collections", "title": "Sets: keep only unique values", "intro": "Sets help you remove duplicates and compare groups.", "body": "<p><code>{1, 2, 2, 3}</code> is the set {1, 2, 3}. Sets have no sequence positions and no guaranteed display order. An empty set is <code>set()</code>; <code>{}</code> makes an empty dictionary.</p><p>Use <code>.add(x)</code> to add an item, <code>.remove(x)</code> to remove an existing item (or raise KeyError), and <code>.discard(x)</code> to remove it if present.</p><p><code>a | b</code> is union: either group. <code>a &amp; b</code> is intersection: both groups. <code>a - b</code> is difference: in a but not b. Membership uses <code>in</code>.</p>", "example": "a = {\"Python\", \"SQL\"}\nb = {\"Python\", \"Java\"}\nprint(a & b) # {\"Python\"}", "task": "Convert names to a set named unique. With group_a and group_b, create both (intersection), everyone (union), and only_a (difference).", "starter": "names = [\"Ada\", \"Lin\", \"Ada\"]\ngroup_a = {\"Ada\", \"Lin\"}\ngroup_b = {\"Lin\", \"Sam\"}\n", "answer": "names = [\"Ada\", \"Lin\", \"Ada\"]\ngroup_a = {\"Ada\", \"Lin\"}\ngroup_b = {\"Lin\", \"Sam\"}\nunique = set(names)\nboth = group_a & group_b\neveryone = group_a | group_b\nonly_a = group_a - group_b", "hint": "The operators are &, |, and -. Results are sets, so their printed order does not matter.", "tests": [{"label": "Remove duplicates", "expr": "unique == {\"Ada\", \"Lin\"}"}, {"label": "Find shared names", "expr": "both == {\"Lin\"}"}, {"label": "Combine the groups", "expr": "everyone == {\"Ada\", \"Lin\", \"Sam\"}"}, {"label": "Find names only in A", "expr": "only_a == {\"Ada\"}"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 13, "group": "Functions", "title": "Write it once, use it again", "intro": "A function gives a reusable set of instructions a name.", "body": "<p>Define a function with <code>def</code>, its name, parentheses, and a colon. Indent its body. Defining a function does not run it: call it with parentheses.</p><p>A <strong>parameter</strong> is a name in the definition. An <strong>argument</strong> is a value you pass when calling. Functions can take several parameters.</p><p><code>return</code> sends a value back and ends that function call. <code>print()</code> only displays a value. Without return, a function returns None.</p><p>Default parameters make arguments optional: <code>def greet(name=\"friend\"):</code>. Keyword arguments name their parameter: <code>greet(name=\"Ada\")</code>. Required parameters go before default parameters.</p>", "example": "def greet(name=\"friend\"):\n    return f\"Hi, {name}!\"\n\nprint(greet())\nprint(greet(name=\"Ada\"))", "task": "Define add(a, b=1) that returns their sum. It should work for positive numbers, negative numbers, and keyword arguments.", "starter": "def add(a, b=1):\n    # Return their sum\n    pass\n", "answer": "def add(a, b=1):\n    return a + b", "hint": "Use return, not print, to make the result available to the caller.", "tests": [{"label": "Add two positive numbers", "expr": "add(2,3) == 5"}, {"label": "Use the default", "expr": "add(4) == 5"}, {"label": "Handle negative values", "expr": "add(-2,1) == -1"}, {"label": "Accept keyword arguments", "expr": "add(b=7,a=2) == 9"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 14, "group": "Functions", "title": "Scope: where names belong", "intro": "A name inside a function usually belongs to that function.", "body": "<p>A variable assigned inside a function is <strong>local</strong> to that function. A variable created at the file’s top level is <strong>global</strong>. Separate function calls have their own local names.</p><p>A function can read an outer name, but assigning to a name inside the function normally makes it local. Trying to read that local name before assigning it can cause UnboundLocalError.</p><p><code>global count</code> lets a function assign to a global count. Use it sparingly. Passing values in and returning results makes code easier to understand and test.</p>", "example": "count = 10\ndef next_count(value):\n    local_result = value + 1\n    return local_result\nprint(next_count(count)) # 11\nprint(count)             # still 10", "task": "Keep total = 10 unchanged. Define increase(value) to return value + 1 using a local variable named result. Save increase(total) in updated.", "starter": "total = 10\ndef increase(value):\n    pass\n", "answer": "total = 10\ndef increase(value):\n    result = value + 1\n    return result\nupdated = increase(total)", "hint": "Use a parameter and return a result; you do not need global.", "tests": [{"label": "Return a new value", "expr": "updated == 11"}, {"label": "Preserve the outer total", "expr": "total == 10"}, {"label": "Works for another value", "expr": "increase(99) == 100"}, {"label": "Keep result local", "expr": "\"result\" not in user_globals"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 15, "group": "Errors", "title": "Read errors without fear", "intro": "Errors are clues about what to try next.", "body": "<p>A <strong>syntax error</strong> means Python cannot understand the code’s structure: perhaps a missing colon or quote. A <strong>runtime error</strong> occurs while valid code executes, such as dividing by zero. A <strong>logic error</strong> runs without an exception but gives the wrong answer.</p><p>Read the last line of an error first: it names the problem. Then find the line number in practice.py. NameError often means a misspelled name; TypeError often means incompatible types; IndexError means a position is out of bounds.</p><p>Debugging means narrowing down the cause. Predict a value, print it, and compare. Change one thing at a time. Try a small example you can calculate by hand.</p>", "example": "# Text + number is a TypeError\n# print(\"Age: \" + 25)\nprint(\"Age: \" + str(25))", "task": "Repair the function so average([10, 20, 30]) returns 20. For an empty list, return 0. The current version incorrectly adds 1 to the divisor.", "starter": "def average(numbers):\n    return sum(numbers) / (len(numbers) + 1)\n", "answer": "def average(numbers):\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n    return sum(numbers) / len(numbers)", "hint": "An average is sum divided by count. Check for an empty list before dividing.", "tests": [{"label": "Average of three numbers", "expr": "average([10,20,30]) == 20"}, {"label": "One number stays itself", "expr": "average([7]) == 7"}, {"label": "Handle an empty list", "expr": "average([]) == 0"}, {"label": "Handle negative numbers", "expr": "average([-4,2]) == -1"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 16, "group": "Errors", "title": "Handle exceptions thoughtfully", "intro": "Recover from an expected problem without hiding every mistake.", "body": "<p>Put risky code in <code>try</code>. A matching <code>except</code> handles a particular exception. Catch specific types such as ValueError or ZeroDivisionError.</p><p><code>else</code> runs when the try block raises no exception. <code>finally</code> runs when leaving the statement, whether it succeeded or failed; it is useful for cleanup.</p><p><code>raise ValueError(\"Age cannot be negative\")</code> reports invalid input deliberately. Avoid catching every error without explaining it: that hides bugs you need to fix.</p>", "example": "try:\n    age = int(\"twenty\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Please enter a whole number.\")\nelse:\n    print(age)\nfinally:\n    print(\"Finished checking\")", "task": "Define safe_int(text): return its integer conversion, or None if conversion raises ValueError or TypeError. Do not return 0 for bad input because 0 is a valid integer.", "starter": "def safe_int(text):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def safe_int(text):\n    try:\n        return int(text)\n    except (ValueError, TypeError):\n        return None", "hint": "A tuple of exception types catches either one: except (ValueError, TypeError):", "tests": [{"label": "Convert valid text", "expr": "safe_int(\"42\") == 42"}, {"label": "Zero stays zero", "expr": "safe_int(\"0\") == 0"}, {"label": "Invalid text is handled", "expr": "safe_int(\"hello\") is None"}, {"label": "None is handled", "expr": "safe_int(None) is None"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 17, "group": "Modules", "title": "Borrow useful tools", "intro": "A module groups reusable Python code.", "body": "<p><code>import math</code> loads a module; use its functions with <code>math.sqrt(9)</code>. <code>from math import sqrt</code> imports a specific name. <code>import math as m</code> gives the module an alias.</p><p>Python’s standard library comes with Python. <code>math</code> offers numeric functions and constants, <code>random</code> handles simulations and games, and <code>datetime</code> handles dates and times.</p><p>A library can contain many modules. Third-party libraries are installed separately; this beginner playground focuses on built-ins and the standard library. For real passwords, use secrets instead of random.</p>", "example": "import math\nfrom datetime import date\nprint(math.sqrt(81))\nprint(date(2026, 1, 2).isoformat())", "task": "Import math. Define hypotenuse(a, b) to return the square root of a² + b². Use math.sqrt() or math.hypot().", "starter": "import math\n\ndef hypotenuse(a, b):\n    pass\n", "answer": "import math\n\ndef hypotenuse(a, b):\n    return math.sqrt(a ** 2 + b ** 2)", "hint": "Square both sides with ** 2, add them, then take the square root.", "tests": [{"label": "A 3–4–5 triangle", "expr": "hypotenuse(3,4) == 5"}, {"label": "A second triangle", "expr": "hypotenuse(5,12) == 13"}, {"label": "Zero-length sides", "expr": "hypotenuse(0,0) == 0"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 18, "group": "Files", "title": "Read & write files", "intro": "Keep information outside a variable.", "body": "<p><code>open(path, mode)</code> opens a file. Modes include <code>\"r\"</code> for reading, <code>\"w\"</code> for writing (replacing old contents), and <code>\"a\"</code> for appending.</p><p>Use <code>with open(...) as file:</code> so the file closes automatically. <code>.read()</code> gets all text, a for loop reads line by line, and <code>.write(text)</code> writes text without adding a newline for you.</p><p>A relative path such as notes.txt starts in the current folder. An absolute path starts at the filesystem root. Use <code>encoding=\"utf-8\"</code> for predictable text handling.</p><p>Here, files exist in a temporary playground filesystem. They never touch your computer’s files and disappear on the next run. Write and read them within the same program.</p>", "example": "with open(\"hello.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    file.write(\"Hello!\\n\")\nwith open(\"hello.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    print(file.read())", "task": "Write Learn Python followed by a newline to notes.txt. Append Practice daily followed by a newline. Read the entire file into a variable named contents.", "starter": "# Write, append, then read notes.txt\n", "answer": "with open(\"notes.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    file.write(\"Learn Python\\n\")\nwith open(\"notes.txt\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    file.write(\"Practice daily\\n\")\nwith open(\"notes.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    contents = file.read()", "hint": "Use three with blocks. The middle one should use mode \"a\".", "tests": [{"label": "Both lines are in the file", "expr": "open(\"notes.txt\").read() == \"Learn Python\\nPractice daily\\n\""}, {"label": "Read into contents", "expr": "contents == \"Learn Python\\nPractice daily\\n\""}, {"label": "Use a with block", "expr": "has_node(\"With\")"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 19, "group": "Functions", "title": "List comprehensions", "intro": "A shorter way to build a list from another sequence.", "body": "<p>A comprehension combines a result expression and a loop: <code>[n * 2 for n in numbers]</code>. Read it as “make n * 2 for each n in numbers.”</p><p>Add a filter after the loop: <code>[n for n in numbers if n &gt; 0]</code>. Only matching items are included.</p><p>First understand the equivalent loop: create an empty list, iterate, and append each result. Use a comprehension when it stays readable, and use ordinary loops for complicated steps or side effects.</p>", "example": "squares = []\nfor n in range(4):\n    squares.append(n ** 2)\n\n# Same result, shorter:\nsquares = [n ** 2 for n in range(4)]", "task": "Using a list comprehension, create even_squares from numbers, keeping only even values and squaring each one. Checks also try negative numbers and an empty list.", "starter": "numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\n", "answer": "numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\neven_squares = [n ** 2 for n in numbers if n % 2 == 0]", "hint": "n % 2 == 0 tests whether n is even.", "tests": [{"label": "Square only even numbers", "expr": "even_squares == [4,16,36]"}, {"label": "Use a comprehension", "expr": "has_node(\"ListComp\")"}, {"label": "Handle negatives and zero", "expr": "rerun({\"numbers\":[-2,0,3]})[\"even_squares\"] == [4,0]"}, {"label": "Handle an empty list", "expr": "rerun({\"numbers\":[]})[\"even_squares\"] == []"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 20, "group": "OOP", "title": "Objects with their own data", "intro": "Bring related information and behavior together.", "body": "<p>An <strong>object</strong> combines data and behavior. Strings and lists are already objects. A <strong>class</strong> defines a new kind of object.</p><p><code>__init__</code> initializes an instance. <code>self</code> refers to that instance. Store attributes with <code>self.name = name</code>. A method is a function defined in a class; Python supplies self when you call it on an instance.</p><p>Create an object with <code>Student(\"Ada\")</code>. Each instance has its own attributes. Basic encapsulation means organizing state and operations together; a leading underscore marks an attribute as internal by convention, not enforced privacy.</p><p>Inheritance reuses a parent class: <code>class Graduate(Student):</code>. The child can add or override methods; <code>super().__init__(name)</code> calls parent initialization. Prefer simple classes until this solves a real need.</p>", "example": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n\n    def greet(self):\n        return f\"Hi, I’m {self.name}\"\n\nada = Student(\"Ada\")\nprint(ada.greet())", "task": "Create a Counter class. Its __init__ sets self.value to 0. Its increment() method adds 1 to self.value and returns the new value. Each counter must keep independent state.", "starter": "class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        pass\n\n    def increment(self):\n        pass\n", "answer": "class Counter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.value = 0\n\n    def increment(self):\n        self.value += 1\n        return self.value", "hint": "Use self.value so the number belongs to the particular counter object.", "tests": [{"label": "Start at zero", "expr": "Counter().value == 0"}, {"label": "First increment returns one", "expr": "Counter().increment() == 1"}, {"label": "Remember repeated increments", "expr": "counter_repeats(Counter)"}, {"label": "Keep counters independent", "expr": "counter_independent(Counter)"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 21, "group": "Reference", "title": "Built-ins you’ll use often", "intro": "A small reference you can return to anytime.", "body": "<p><code>print(x)</code> displays x; <code>input()</code> reads text. <code>type(x)</code> inspects its type. <code>int()</code>, <code>float()</code>, <code>str()</code>, and <code>bool()</code> convert values.</p><p><code>len(items)</code> counts items. <code>range(start, stop, step)</code> creates an integer sequence excluding stop. <code>sum(numbers)</code>, <code>min(numbers)</code>, and <code>max(numbers)</code> summarize numeric collections. min and max need an item or an explicit default.</p><p><code>sorted(items)</code> returns a sorted list. <code>enumerate(items, start=1)</code> yields position-value pairs. <code>zip(a, b)</code> pairs corresponding items, stopping at the shorter input by default.</p>", "example": "names = [\"Ada\", \"Lin\"]\nscores = [90, 80]\nfor position, name in enumerate(names, start=1):\n    print(position, name)\nprint(list(zip(names, scores)))", "task": "From scores, calculate total, lowest, highest, and ordered (a new sorted list). Use zip() to create pairs of names and scores, and convert the result to a list.", "starter": "scores = [90, 70, 80]\nnames = [\"Ada\", \"Lin\", \"Sam\"]\n", "answer": "scores = [90, 70, 80]\nnames = [\"Ada\", \"Lin\", \"Sam\"]\ntotal = sum(scores)\nlowest = min(scores)\nhighest = max(scores)\nordered = sorted(scores)\npairs = list(zip(names, scores))", "hint": "Each summary function takes scores as its argument. sorted() leaves scores unchanged.", "tests": [{"label": "Summarize the scores", "expr": "total == 240 and lowest == 70 and highest == 90"}, {"label": "Sort without changing scores", "expr": "ordered == [70,80,90] and scores == [90,70,80]"}, {"label": "Pair corresponding items", "expr": "pairs == [(\"Ada\",90),(\"Lin\",70),(\"Sam\",80)]"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 22, "group": "Projects", "title": "Temperature converter", "intro": "Project 01 · A first useful calculation.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Use arithmetic and a function. Fahrenheit = Celsius × 9 / 5 + 32. Extension: ask which direction to convert.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def to_fahrenheit(celsius):\n    return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32", "task": "Define to_fahrenheit(celsius) that returns the converted temperature.", "starter": "def to_fahrenheit(celsius):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def to_fahrenheit(celsius):\n    return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32", "hint": "Use parentheses if they help you see the formula.", "tests": [{"label": "Freezing point", "expr": "to_fahrenheit(0) == 32"}, {"label": "Boiling point", "expr": "to_fahrenheit(100) == 212"}, {"label": "A negative temperature", "expr": "to_fahrenheit(-40) == -40"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 23, "group": "Projects", "title": "Calculator", "intro": "Project 02 · Combine functions and decisions.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Use conditions to select the requested operation. Extension: read two numbers and an operator with input().</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def calculate(a, b, op):\n    if op == \"+\":\n        return a + b\n    if op == \"-\":\n        return a - b\n    if op == \"*\":\n        return a * b\n    if op == \"/\" and b != 0:\n        return a / b\n    return None", "task": "Define calculate(a, b, op) for +, -, *, and /. Return None for division by zero or an unknown operator.", "starter": "def calculate(a, b, op):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def calculate(a, b, op):\n    if op == \"+\":\n        return a + b\n    if op == \"-\":\n        return a - b\n    if op == \"*\":\n        return a * b\n    if op == \"/\" and b != 0:\n        return a / b\n    return None", "hint": "Check op using ==. Guard division with b != 0.", "tests": [{"label": "Addition", "expr": "calculate(2,3,\"+\") == 5"}, {"label": "Subtraction", "expr": "calculate(2,3,\"-\") == -1"}, {"label": "Multiplication", "expr": "calculate(-2,3,\"*\") == -6"}, {"label": "Division", "expr": "calculate(7,2,\"/\") == 3.5"}, {"label": "Division by zero", "expr": "calculate(7,0,\"/\") is None"}, {"label": "Unknown operator", "expr": "calculate(2,3,\"?\") is None"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 24, "group": "Projects", "title": "Grade calculator", "intro": "Project 03 · Practice exact boundaries.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Conditions must include the boundary values. Extension: reject scores outside 0–100.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def letter_grade(score):\n    if score >= 90: return \"A\"\n    if score >= 80: return \"B\"\n    if score >= 70: return \"C\"\n    if score >= 60: return \"D\"\n    return \"F\"", "task": "Define letter_grade(score): A for 90+, B for 80–89, C for 70–79, D for 60–69, F below 60.", "starter": "def letter_grade(score):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def letter_grade(score):\n    if score >= 90: return \"A\"\n    if score >= 80: return \"B\"\n    if score >= 70: return \"C\"\n    if score >= 60: return \"D\"\n    return \"F\"", "hint": "Test larger thresholds before smaller ones.", "tests": [{"label": "A boundary", "expr": "letter_grade(90) == \"A\""}, {"label": "B boundary", "expr": "letter_grade(80) == \"B\""}, {"label": "C boundary", "expr": "letter_grade(70) == \"C\""}, {"label": "D boundary", "expr": "letter_grade(60) == \"D\""}, {"label": "Below passing", "expr": "letter_grade(59) == \"F\""}, {"label": "Maximum", "expr": "letter_grade(100) == \"A\""}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 25, "group": "Projects", "title": "Number guessing game", "intro": "Project 04 · Give a player useful feedback.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Separate feedback from the game loop. Extension: choose random.randint(1, 100), then loop until the player guesses it.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def feedback(guess, secret):\n    if guess < secret: return \"Too low\"\n    if guess > secret: return \"Too high\"\n    return \"Correct!\"", "task": "Define feedback(guess, secret), returning Too low, Too high, or Correct! exactly.", "starter": "def feedback(guess, secret):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def feedback(guess, secret):\n    if guess < secret: return \"Too low\"\n    if guess > secret: return \"Too high\"\n    return \"Correct!\"", "hint": "Compare the guess with the secret in each direction.", "tests": [{"label": "Low guess", "expr": "feedback(2,5) == \"Too low\""}, {"label": "High guess", "expr": "feedback(8,5) == \"Too high\""}, {"label": "Correct guess", "expr": "feedback(5,5) == \"Correct!\""}, {"label": "Different secret", "expr": "feedback(50,90) == \"Too low\""}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 26, "group": "Projects", "title": "Rock-paper-scissors", "intro": "Project 05 · Model three simple rules.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Rock beats scissors, scissors beats paper, and paper beats rock. Extension: choose the computer move with random.choice().</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def winner(player, computer):\n    if player == computer: return \"draw\"\n    wins = {(\"rock\", \"scissors\"), (\"scissors\", \"paper\"), (\"paper\", \"rock\")}\n    return \"win\" if (player, computer) in wins else \"lose\"", "task": "Define winner(player, computer). For lowercase rock, paper, and scissors, return win, lose, or draw.", "starter": "def winner(player, computer):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def winner(player, computer):\n    if player == computer: return \"draw\"\n    wins = {(\"rock\", \"scissors\"), (\"scissors\", \"paper\"), (\"paper\", \"rock\")}\n    return \"win\" if (player, computer) in wins else \"lose\"", "hint": "Store winning pairs in a set of tuples.", "tests": [{"label": "All draws", "expr": "all(winner(x,x) == \"draw\" for x in [\"rock\",\"paper\",\"scissors\"])"}, {"label": "All winning pairs", "expr": "all(winner(a,b) == \"win\" for a,b in [(\"rock\",\"scissors\"),(\"paper\",\"rock\"),(\"scissors\",\"paper\")])"}, {"label": "All losing pairs", "expr": "all(winner(a,b) == \"lose\" for a,b in [(\"scissors\",\"rock\"),(\"rock\",\"paper\"),(\"paper\",\"scissors\")])"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 27, "group": "Projects", "title": "Password generator", "intro": "Project 06 · Turn repetition into text.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Use secrets.choice() to choose characters and \"\".join() to combine them. This exercise checks format, not password strength. Extension: add digits and punctuation and choose a longer length.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "import secrets", "task": "Define make_password(length) returning that many characters from abcXYZ123!. Return an empty string for length 0. Use the secrets module.", "starter": "import secrets\n\ndef make_password(length):\n    pass\n", "answer": "import secrets\n\ndef make_password(length):\n    alphabet = \"abcXYZ123!\"\n    return \"\".join(secrets.choice(alphabet) for _ in range(length))", "hint": "A generator expression inside join() can repeat secrets.choice() length times.", "tests": [{"label": "Requested length", "expr": "len(make_password(12)) == 12"}, {"label": "Allowed characters", "expr": "set(make_password(100)) <= set(\"abcXYZ123!\")"}, {"label": "Empty password", "expr": "make_password(0) == \"\""}, {"label": "Single character", "expr": "len(make_password(1)) == 1"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 28, "group": "Projects", "title": "To-do list", "intro": "Project 07 · Manage a changing list.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Return a new list so callers keep their original data. Extension: add a menu with add, view, and complete commands.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def add_task(tasks, task):\n    return tasks + [task]", "task": "Define add_task(tasks, task) returning a new list with task added. Define complete_task(tasks, task) returning a new list without the first matching task; if absent, leave the contents unchanged.", "starter": "def add_task(tasks, task):\n    pass\n\ndef complete_task(tasks, task):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def add_task(tasks, task):\n    return tasks + [task]\n\ndef complete_task(tasks, task):\n    result = tasks.copy()\n    if task in result:\n        result.remove(task)\n    return result", "hint": "Use .copy() before removing an item.", "tests": [{"label": "Add a task", "expr": "add_task([\"Study\"],\"Rest\") == [\"Study\",\"Rest\"]"}, {"label": "Complete a task", "expr": "complete_task([\"Study\",\"Rest\"],\"Study\") == [\"Rest\"]"}, {"label": "Missing task", "expr": "complete_task([\"Study\"],\"Walk\") == [\"Study\"]"}, {"label": "Remove first duplicate only", "expr": "complete_task([\"Study\",\"Study\"],\"Study\") == [\"Study\"]"}, {"label": "Original lists stay unchanged", "expr": "todo_preserves(add_task,complete_task)"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 29, "group": "Projects", "title": "Quiz program", "intro": "Project 08 · Compare answers fairly.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Normalize whitespace and case before comparing answers. Extension: loop over a dictionary of questions and total correct answers.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def is_correct(answer, expected):\n    return answer.strip().lower() == expected.strip().lower()", "task": "Define is_correct(answer, expected) returning True when both strings match after trimming outer spaces and ignoring case.", "starter": "def is_correct(answer, expected):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def is_correct(answer, expected):\n    return answer.strip().lower() == expected.strip().lower()", "hint": "Apply strip().lower() to both strings.", "tests": [{"label": "Ignore case", "expr": "is_correct(\"PYTHON\",\"Python\") is True"}, {"label": "Ignore outer spaces", "expr": "is_correct(\"  Python \",\"python\") is True"}, {"label": "Reject wrong answer", "expr": "is_correct(\"Java\",\"Python\") is False"}, {"label": "Normalize expected answer too", "expr": "is_correct(\"python\",\" PYTHON \") is True"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 30, "group": "Projects", "title": "Contact book", "intro": "Project 09 · Find the right information.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Dictionaries make lookups simple. Extension: add commands to update and remove contacts, then save them in a text file.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def lookup(contacts, name):\n    return contacts.get(name, \"Not found\")", "task": "Define lookup(contacts, name) returning the associated phone string, or Not found for an unknown name. Do not modify contacts.", "starter": "def lookup(contacts, name):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def lookup(contacts, name):\n    return contacts.get(name, \"Not found\")", "hint": "Use .get() with a default.", "tests": [{"label": "Existing contact", "expr": "lookup({\"Ada\":\"123\"},\"Ada\") == \"123\""}, {"label": "Unknown contact", "expr": "lookup({\"Ada\":\"123\"},\"Lin\") == \"Not found\""}, {"label": "Empty book", "expr": "lookup({},\"Ada\") == \"Not found\""}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 31, "group": "Projects", "title": "Expense tracker", "intro": "Project 10 · Summarize a list of records.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Each expense is a dictionary with category and amount. Extension: accept entries, save them, and show a total by category.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def total_for(expenses, category):\n    total = 0\n    for expense in expenses:\n        if expense[\"category\"] == category:\n            total += expense[\"amount\"]\n    return total", "task": "Define total_for(expenses, category), summing amounts for the matching category. Return 0 when no entries match.", "starter": "def total_for(expenses, category):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def total_for(expenses, category):\n    total = 0\n    for expense in expenses:\n        if expense[\"category\"] == category:\n            total += expense[\"amount\"]\n    return total", "hint": "Loop over expenses and add only matching amounts.", "tests": [{"label": "Sum matching entries", "expr": "total_for([{\"category\":\"food\",\"amount\":5},{\"category\":\"food\",\"amount\":7},{\"category\":\"travel\",\"amount\":20}],\"food\") == 12"}, {"label": "No matches", "expr": "total_for([{\"category\":\"food\",\"amount\":5}],\"travel\") == 0"}, {"label": "Empty list", "expr": "total_for([],\"food\") == 0"}, {"label": "Decimal amounts", "expr": "abs(total_for([{\"category\":\"food\",\"amount\":2.5}],\"food\") - 2.5) < 1e-9"}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 32, "group": "Projects", "title": "Text adventure", "intro": "Project 11 · A tiny world with choices.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Model a game as a state plus a choice. Extension: loop, display the room, and ask which direction to move.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "def move(room, direction):\n    if room == \"hall\" and direction == \"north\":\n        return \"library\"\n    if room == \"library\" and direction == \"south\":\n        return \"hall\"\n    return room", "task": "Define move(room, direction): hall + north leads to library; library + south leads to hall. Any other choice keeps the same room.", "starter": "def move(room, direction):\n    pass\n", "answer": "def move(room, direction):\n    if room == \"hall\" and direction == \"north\":\n        return \"library\"\n    if room == \"library\" and direction == \"south\":\n        return \"hall\"\n    return room", "hint": "Use and to match a room and direction together.", "tests": [{"label": "Enter the library", "expr": "move(\"hall\",\"north\") == \"library\""}, {"label": "Return to hall", "expr": "move(\"library\",\"south\") == \"hall\""}, {"label": "Blocked direction", "expr": "move(\"hall\",\"west\") == \"hall\""}, {"label": "Another blocked direction", "expr": "move(\"library\",\"north\") == \"library\""}], "inputs": ""}, {"id": 33, "group": "Projects", "title": "Student grade manager", "intro": "Project 12 · Put your fundamentals together.", "body": "<p><strong>Build in three steps.</strong> First write the core logic and pass the checks. Then call it with your own values and print the results. Finally add input() and a loop to make it interactive.</p><p>Combine classes, lists, and methods. Extension: create a dictionary of Student objects, add a command menu, and export a summary to a file.</p><p>The checks below verify the core behavior. The interactive interface is your open-ended extension: experiment with prompts, wording, and extra features.</p>", "example": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        self.grades = []", "task": "Define Student(name), storing name and an empty grades list. add_grade(score) appends a grade. average() returns the mean or 0 for no grades. Each student must have their own grades.", "starter": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        pass\n\n    def add_grade(self, score):\n        pass\n\n    def average(self):\n        pass\n", "answer": "class Student:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        self.grades = []\n\n    def add_grade(self, score):\n        self.grades.append(score)\n\n    def average(self):\n        if not self.grades:\n            return 0\n        return sum(self.grades) / len(self.grades)", "hint": "Create self.grades inside __init__, so each object gets its own list.", "tests": [{"label": "Store the name", "expr": "Student(\"Ada\").name == \"Ada\""}, {"label": "Empty average", "expr": "Student(\"Ada\").average() == 0"}, {"label": "Add and average grades", "expr": "student_average(Student)"}, {"label": "Independent student records", "expr": "student_independent(Student)"}], "inputs": ""}];
